Here's Step 2, broken into an exact sequence your backend person(s) can execute without guesswork. I'll give the commands, the folder structure, and the actual file contents for the skeleton.

## 2.1 — Initialize the project

```bash
mkdir server && cd server
npm init -y
npm install express prisma @prisma/client dotenv bcrypt jsonwebtoken multer cors
npm install -D typescript ts-node-dev @types/express @types/node @types/bcrypt @types/jsonwebtoken @types/multer @types/cors
npx tsc --init
```

## 2.2 — Folder structure to create

```
server/
├── prisma/
│   └── schema.prisma        ← paste the validated schema here
├── src/
│   ├── routes/
│   ├── controllers/
│   ├── middleware/
│   ├── utils/
│   ├── app.ts
│   └── server.ts
├── uploads/                 ← for CSR/Challenge proof files
├── .env
├── .gitignore
├── tsconfig.json
└── package.json
```

Commands:
```bash
mkdir -p src/routes src/controllers src/middleware src/utils uploads
```

## 2.3 — Configure `tsconfig.json`

Replace the generated file with this (lean config, matches Node/Express needs):

```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2020",
    "module": "commonjs",
    "outDir": "./dist",
    "rootDir": "./src",
    "strict": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*"]
}
```

## 2.4 — Set up `.env` and `.gitignore`

`.env`:
```
DATABASE_URL="file:./dev.db"
JWT_SECRET="replace-with-a-random-string"
PORT=4000
```

`.gitignore`:
```
node_modules/
dist/
.env
prisma/dev.db
uploads/*
!uploads/.gitkeep
```

**Important**: your Prisma schema file goes in `prisma/schema.prisma` — paste the validated schema from the last step there now, and add `env("DATABASE_URL")` is already wired since that's already in the schema's `datasource` block.

## 2.5 — Run the first migration

```bash
npx prisma migrate dev --name init
```

This creates `dev.db` (SQLite file), applies your schema, and generates the Prisma Client. Confirm it worked:

```bash
npx prisma studio
```

This opens a browser GUI showing all your tables empty but present — if this loads and shows every model from the schema, migration succeeded.

## 2.6 — `src/app.ts` (Express app setup)

```typescript
import express from 'express';
import cors from 'cors';
import healthRoute from './routes/health.route';

const app = express();

app.use(cors());
app.use(express.json());
app.use('/uploads', express.static('uploads'));

app.use('/api/health', healthRoute);

export default app;
```

## 2.7 — `src/server.ts` (entry point)

```typescript
import dotenv from 'dotenv';
dotenv.config();

import app from './app';

const PORT = process.env.PORT || 4000;

app.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`Server running on http://localhost:${PORT}`);
});
```

## 2.8 — `src/routes/health.route.ts` (the health-check route)

```typescript
import { Router } from 'express';
import { PrismaClient } from '@prisma/client';

const router = Router();
const prisma = new PrismaClient();

router.get('/', async (req, res) => {
  try {
    // simple query to confirm DB connection is alive, not just the server
    await prisma.$queryRaw`SELECT 1`;
    res.status(200).json({ status: 'ok', db: 'connected' });
  } catch (err) {
    res.status(500).json({ status: 'error', db: 'disconnected' });
  }
});

export default router;
```

## 2.9 — Add dev script to `package.json`

Add this under `"scripts"`:
```json
"scripts": {
  "dev": "ts-node-dev --respawn --transpile-only src/server.ts",
  "build": "tsc",
  "start": "node dist/server.js"
}
```

## 2.10 — Verify everything works

```bash
npm run dev
```

Then in a browser or via curl:
```bash
curl http://localhost:4000/api/health
```

Expected response: `{"status":"ok","db":"connected"}` — this confirms three things simultaneously: TypeScript compiles, Express serves requests, and Prisma can actually talk to your SQLite database. If this returns cleanly, Step 2 is done and Step 3 (Auth) can start immediately.

---

## Checklist to confirm before moving to Step 3
- [ ] `npm run dev` starts with no errors
- [ ] `/api/health` returns `db: "connected"`
- [ ] `npx prisma studio` shows all your models (tables) present and empty
- [ ] `.env` and `dev.db` are NOT committed to git (check `git status` shows them ignored)
- [ ] Folder structure matches above, so Persons 2-4 know exactly where to add their routes/controllers later

Once this is confirmed working, hand it to whoever's doing Step 3 (auth) — they build directly on top of this `app.ts`/`server.ts` foundation without needing to touch the setup again.
